### feat(ops): replay-chain --checkpoint — seed state at a checkpoint DB, verify, replay C+1..end (V-5)

- `scripts/ops/replay-chain.py` gains `--checkpoint DB`: a state-bearing chain.db whose tip C seeds the replay target wholesale (the equivalent of a node that stopped at C resuming sync). Three proofs gate the first import — lineage (checkpoint head hash == snapshot block C, and C+1 names it parent), state-bearing (the checkpoint's account table recomputes to block C's recorded state_root, same input set as `compute_state_root_full`), and a post-`init_db` re-seed root check (catches ensure_bond_accounts-style seeding mutations). `--genesis` is unused in checkpoint mode.
- `--to-height` now actually bounds the run: the stub RPC caps the head/range it serves at the requested height instead of importing to the real tip and failing the expected-count check.
- `--compare-state-with DB` adds the V-5 ladder's layer 2: after a clean replay to the next checkpoint's tip, every non-history table is compared wholesale — covering aux consensus state (escrow, chain_parameter, bridge rows, contracts) that the account-only state root never sees. Volatile locally-stamped columns (`created_at`/`updated_at`) and the replay-generated undo journal are excluded; the compare DB's tip must equal the replay end.
- `tests/unit/test_replay_chain.py`: 29 cases — arg parsing, stub capping, checkpoint head reading, all proof failure modes (tip≥end, lineage/fork mismatch, missing root, empty accounts, root mismatch), copy placement, and wholesale-compare semantics incl. aux-table divergence and volatile-column exclusion.
- `docs/ops/v5-replay-inventory.md`: recipe updated to the implemented flags (`--checkpoint` + `--snapshot`; the planned `--from-height` did not get implemented — the checkpoint file's own tip is the start height and cannot disagree with its state).


### feat(db): opt-in keeper connection (DB_KEEPER_CONNECTION) — stops the close-time WAL checkpoint stalls

- Why: on 2026-10-01 node2's v9 attestation check hit `database is locked` at height 30415 and declined to attest (fail-closed), and its propose loop logged repeated `Failed to propose block: database is locked`. A `/proc/locks` sampler on node2 (10:03-10:28Z) found 32 holds of 2 s or more, all by node2's own RPC and node processes and none by an outside process; the 10:07:32Z failure sat inside an 11.5 s EXCLUSIVE hold by the RPC process.
- Mechanism: every session opens its own connection (NullPool), so whenever none is open the closing one is the last, and SQLite checkpoints and deletes the WAL under an EXCLUSIVE lock while it syncs the disk. node2's WAL is deleted and recreated several times per 30 s. node0/node1/node2 are containers on one machine with chain.db on btrfs over spinning disks: a 4 KB write plus fsync takes a median of ~220 ms with outliers over 1.7 s, against 2 ms on hub and hub1 (ext4). A connection opened during the exclusive hold waits Python's default 5 s (the 60 s busy timeout is set only after the first pragma) and fails.
- Change: `settings.db_keeper_connection` (env `DB_KEEPER_CONNECTION`, default off). When on, `init_db` opens one idle connection per chain and `shutdown_db` closes it, so no session is ever the last one: the WAL stays on disk, is only checkpointed passively, and the exclusive lock is never taken. Failing to open it only logs a warning. Scratch check: with the last connection closed the WAL is deleted; with an idle keeper open it persists.
- Operational note: while it is on, `chain.db` alone is not the whole database; copy it with `sqlite3 .backup` or with the node stopped, never a bare `cp`, and open only `.backup` copies with `immutable=1` (replay-chain already requires a copy).
- Tests: `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_db_keeper.py` (7 cases; the four behaviour tests fail with the `init_db` hook removed). Rollout is per host by env flag, node0 first; the bounded attestation retry from `a5b72f4605` covers holds up to ~10 s and is a partial mitigation next to this.


### fix(rpc): refuse authority-gated types from the wrong sender at mempool admission (V-8)

- Found 1 Oct during the K-1 executor rotation: a GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE from the retired executor `0x02B8…` was admitted to the mempool and only dropped when hub proposed it (`Failed to apply transaction … is not an authorized executor`, 14:21:54 CEST). Nothing was written, but each such transaction costs a mempool slot and a proposer cycle, and any funded account can send one. `_validate_transaction_admission` is the one door for REST, gossip ingest and the p2p transport, and it checked chain, sender, balance and nonce, not who may send a type.
- New `state/admission_authority.py::sender_authority_error` mirrors the two sender gates of `validate_transaction` (GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE against `governance_executors`; ESCROW_RELEASE and ESCROW_REFUND against `escrow_settlement_authority`): the same parameter lookups, version rules (fail-closed from v5, lenient below) and messages, evaluated for the version and height of the next block. The admission function calls it last, so every earlier rejection keeps its message. `validate_transaction` and the apply path are untouched.
- Not covered: BOND_SLASH, whose authority check lives in the apply function and not in `validate_transaction`.
- Behaviour to know: a transaction from a newly added executor or settlement authority is refused at admission until the parameter-changing transaction has sealed, where apply could have accepted it later in the same block. The K-1 rotation already waited for each seal.
- Tests: `tests/test_admission_authority.py`, 57 cases. With only the hook removed the five refusal cases fail; a 45-case matrix (five block versions, three types, three parameter states, signed transactions) compares the helper with `validate_transaction` and fails on any disagreement in outcome or message. The whole `apps/blockchain-node/tests` suite run the way CI does gives the same 6 failures on the parent and on the patch (4 in `test_fleet_digest_spec.py`, 1 in `test_v8_stamped_version.py`, 1 in `security/test_state_transition.py`, all present before) and 57 more passes. Not deployed.


### Consensus: v10 GPU lifecycle gates + attestation observability (2026-10-01)

- `GPU_DEREGISTER` lets a GPU's registrant deactivate it (`5a0e833492`),
  gated at the pinned v10 height 32100 (`02bca02b49`, default recorded in
  the operator docs at `9ed3611280`). `GPU_RETIRED_IDS` refuses
  `GPU_REGISTER`/`GPU_ALLOCATE` for retired ids at admission (`1abd2f14fb`)
  and for blocks from v10 (`c06b1876f8`); `GPU_ALLOCATE` of a deactivated
  GPU is refused at admission (`0eecf6dd59`). `gpu-registry-sweep` repairs
  databases that replayed the retired-GPU rows (`b154bcb06a`).
- Attestation path: each validator now counts once toward quorum
  (`97849fcf59`), a post-quorum linger lets slow attestations land
  (`17bcce06cc`), and both publish and arrival are logged
  (`5613a1442d`, `337b763bd5`). Missed-proposer-round warnings fire only at
  round >= 2 — round 1 is the documented empty-heartbeat landing spot
  (`87c9909370`).
- `consensus_state.save_state()` never committed: the insert was rolled
  back on every call while returning True. It now commits and also saves
  on every validator-set mutation (`0feb7ddfc3`).
- Perf/correctness: scalar tip query in the v9 attestation check
  (`be15bb5d3`), one retry on a locked database (`a5b72f4605`), and
  unbounded `Block` entity loads replaced by scalar/count queries
  (`c075daff9f`).


## v0.25.8

### 2026-09-28 — fork-recovery drills: delta-journal undo verified against real data (fix in this batch)

The non-empty-undo path (SYNC_FORK_UNDO_ENABLED, default on) had never
executed against real production data — only unit and differential tests.
Two drills closed that gap:

**Drill A (copy of node2 chain.db):** reverted a 146-block real segment
(26724..26869, all journal_status=complete) — ancestor state root and the
gpu_registration side-table digest both verified, all block/tx/journal
rows removed. All 5 orphaned txs re-admitted through full admission into
the mempool (signed envelopes, nonces/balances valid post-revert).
Fabricated lost-tx lifecycle verified end-to-end: unsigned orphan ->
admission reject -> sync_fork_orphaned_tx_lost_total +1 ->
sync_fork_domain_rows_orphaned_total +1 -> stake row orphaned -> insert
confirmed lock -> _revive_confirmed_domain_rows restored it to active.

**Drill B (live node0):** sent canary TRANSFER 0xcf2282 (confirmed
26881), stopped the node, reverted [26881..26882] on the live chain.db
via revert_losing_segment (ancestor root verified), requeued the orphan
through real admission, restarted. node0 bulk-pulled the identical
winning branch (0xefca65aacc0feebd), canary re-confirmed at 26881,
fleet reconverged hash-identical at 26886 with zero fork/divergence
errors. Post-import reconcile classified the orphan on-chain -> not
lost.

Findings along the way:

- MEMPOOL_BACKEND drift fixed: node0's blockchain.env had 'memory'
  (fleet: 'database', explicit on hub/hub1/node2, default on node1).
  Normalized to database — node + rpc restarted, running env verified,
  node reconverged. node0 never proposes so the pool is intake-only
  either way, but now matches the fleet and persists across restarts.
  Backup: /etc/aitbc/blockchain.env.bak-20260928-mempool-backend.
- CLI canary note: 'aitbc transactions send --type GPU_REGISTER' fails
  apply with 'must have value=0' since the CLI requires --amount>0;
  the earlier gpu canary used the raw signed-RPC path.
- bridge_block_header digest-cap concern resolved: written only by the
  bridge finalizer (own session), can never enter a block journal, so
  the 100k digest cap is unreachable for it. Code comment added at
  _DIGEST_EXCLUDE; behavior kept fail-closed if ever journaled.
- Pre-existing stale mempool row 0xde13acc on node0's postgres mempool
  (legacy format, no tx_hash in content) — predates the drill, left as
  found.


### 2026-09-28 — consensus replay hardening (signature gate + historical fixture)

- `compute_state_delta` signature verification is now gated to `block_version >= 7`. The v2-v6 parallel era rewrote same-sender nonces before delta computation, so a signature carried in from that era would have been checked against the rewritten nonce — failing a block that applied fine historically. `a3ef90501`
- New `test_historical_replay.py` fixture replays one block per era (v5 same-sender pair through the parallel path, v5 bridge credit, v7 GPU_REGISTER, v8 stamp) via the real import path with frozen state roots. Run against the ungated code it reproduced the exact predicted failure (Invalid signature on the rewritten tx2, state-root mismatch, block rejected).
- New `scripts/ops/replay-chain.py`: replays a chain-db snapshot through `ChainSync.bulk_import_from` over a stub HTTP source (`/rpc/head`, `/rpc/blocks-range`), reporting the first divergent height. Multi-validator attestation/schedule gates are disabled for historical replay (validator set rotated since); proposer-signature and state-root checks stay on. Full hub-snapshot replay currently blocks on reconstructing the exact height-0 account set behind root `0x448a...` — a data-provenance gap, not a code failure.
- Fork undo now drops `account_balance:{chain}:{addr}` / `account_details:{chain}:{addr}` Redis keys for every account the journal touched, committed post-revert in the resolver path — RPC can no longer serve losing-branch balances for the cache TTL. `c18bcc5f5`

### Finding — genesis bootstrap is stale fleet-wide

Every live `genesis.json` describes an older genesis (`0x40ea48f9...`), not the
running chain (`0xd95e600b...`), and `/rpc/genesis_allocations` returns
"metadata not found" because block 0 embeds no allocations. A fresh node
cannot bootstrap from genesis today — all current nodes were joined via DB
snapshots. The exact height-0 allocation set producing recorded root
`0x448a...` was overwritten during the Sep-9 reset run; reconstructing it is
the remaining prerequisite for full-history hub replay.


